4.43 also when karam, kṛtam, kṛdhi, karat, [or] kaḥ follow;

4.44 and [before] pari standing at the end of a verse-quarter;

4.46 [and visarga in] vāstoḥ when the word pati follows;

4.47 in āviḥ, haviḥ, [and] jyotiḥ, if k follows;

4.48 likewise the two words pānta and paśyanti;

4.49 [and in] iḷāyāḥ, gāḥ, namasaḥ, devayuḥ, druhaḥ, mātuḥ, [and] iḷaḥ, when these occur as word-readings;

4.53 before the word pathi: jinvataḥ, cetathaḥ, [and] mahaḥ;

4.54 before the word pṛthu: viśvataḥ, vīḷitaḥ, [and] rajaḥ;

4.55 before the words kāma, poṣa, and pūrdhi: rāyaḥ;

4.56 divaḥ at the beginning and at the end of a verse-quarter, and before pari;

4.57 divaḥ before pṛthivyāḥ, and adhamaḥ before padīṣṭa, if the former is at the beginning of a verse-quarter;

4.61 kabandham, pṛthu, kaṇvāsaḥ, putraḥ, pātu, pathā, payaḥ, pāyuḥ, pṛṣṭham, padam—the occurrences of these when divaḥ follows;

4.62 rajasaḥ pāti, antaspathāḥ, kaḥ kāvyā, caturaḥ kara, svāduḥ ila, nidaḥ pātu, dyauḥ pitaḥ, vasatiḥ kṛtā;

4.63 tapoḥ pavitram, triḥ pūtvī, dīḥ pīpāya, vibhiḥ patāt, dyauḥ pitā, rajasaḥ pṛṣṭaḥ, daduḥ pajrāya, naḥ karaḥ;

4.64 vasuḥ kuvit, manuḥ pitā, pituḥ pitā, pituḥ pari, praṇaḥ puraḥ, mayaḥ karan, nabhaḥ payaḥ, trayaḥ paraḥ;

4.65 The sound n preceded by ā, even at the end of a word, when followed by a vowel, is elided,

4.66 [in] ajrān, jagrasānān, jaghanvān, devahūtamān, badbadhānān indra, somān, tṛṣāṇān naḥ, deva devān, and hanta devān;

4.67 these are the word-occurrences ending in -ān;

4.68 and where hiatus is intended: pīvoannān rayivṛdhaḥ, dadhanvān yaḥ, jujuvān yaḥ, svavān yātu, [and] dadvān vā;

4.69 in hatam yonau vacobhiḥ, yān, yuvanyūn, [and] vaniṣīṣṭa, preceded by ī or ū, it becomes r before these [words],

4.71 and in dasyūn ekaḥ, and nṝn abhi;

4.72 these are the stop-to-r combinations (sparśarephasandhi);

4.73 not in asmān upa, etāvān, sphurān, gacchān, devān ayāṭ, vahān, hiraṇyacakrān, māyāvān, ghoṣān, tān aśvinā, avidvān, payasvān, putrān ā dhehi, āyajīyān, patīn, [and] uroḥ;

[The combinations] *tān te*, *sarvān tān*, *devān tvam*, *tān trāyasva*, *āvadan tvam ca*,

have visarga when the following [letters] follow; these are the combinations of stops and spirants.

[In] *nṝn patibhyaḥ*, *nṝn praṇetram*, *nṝn pātram*, *svatavān pāyuḥ*, this euphonic combination is strictly separated (*vikrānta*);

Upon the elision, conversion to r (*rephabhāva*), or conversion to a spirant (*ūṣmabhāva*) of n, the vowel preceding its place becomes nasalized;

and the initial vowel of following words, even within a word.

[So also] in *māṃspacanyā*, *māṃścatve*, and in *maṃścatoḥ*.

In the word *īm*, elision of the final letter [occurs] before these following words: *garbham*, *gāvaḥ*, *vatsam*, *mṛjanti*, *pṛcyate*, *sakhāyaḥ*, *vivyāca*, *punaḥ*, *riṇanti*, [and] *ratham*; that is indeed a syllable-following euphonic combination.

When *puru*, *pṛthu*, or *adhi* precedes, or after a short vowel at the end of a preceding word, ś arises in between before the word *candra*;

in the word *pari*, ṣ arises before forms of *kṛ*;

in *vana*, an r arises before the following word *sad*;

[as in] *pariṣkṛṇvanti vedhasaḥ*,

These euphonic combinations of pure syllables have been stated.

In [the verses of] Medhātithi, [words] ending in *varuṇa* and ending in *vrata*, when followed by stops or semivowels, are shortened;

[also] in *ādityā devā varuṇā asurā*, in the passage beginning with *yā*, and so forth;

[also in] *jugukṣataḥ*, *dudukṣan gāḥ*, *adukṣat*, *duakṣan vṛdhe*, *asya dukṣata*, *anu dakṣi*, *dakṣat na patman*, *dakṣuṣaḥ*, *abhi dakṣat*, *kṛṣṇāsaḥ*, [and] *dakṣi hiyānasya dakṣoḥ*.

Inside a verse-quarter, s preceded by an altering vowel becomes ṣ, even with spirants following, as has been stated; and after an e with other [intervening sounds].

One should know that here retroflexion comes first; from that, modification (*vyāpatti*) occurs.

[Retroflexion of initial s occurs after] *su*, *ūtī*, *nakiḥ*, *svaiḥ*, *vi*, *uru*, *nahi*, *abhi*, *trī*, *ni*, [and] *hi*;

[and] after a disyllable, in *sat* and *sthaḥ*;

However, the dental class (*t-varga*) shifts to the retroflex class (*ṭ-varga*) when preceded by ṣ, even inside a word.

[No change occurs in] *sitām*, *sadhasthāt*, *stanihi*, *stavāma*, *stave*, *stuvanti*, *stuhi*, *sīm*, *stuta*, *stha*, *sāhi*, *sta*, *stup*, *satsi*, *satsat*, *svani*, [forms beginning with] *stobha-*, and so forth, even after polysyllabic endings;

[also in] *sedha*, *svāpaya*, *sasvaje*, *sasvajāte*, and *sasāda*;

and following s [remains unchanged] in *santam*, *santaḥ*, *santi*, *pūrvīḥ*, *sthuḥ*, *sthāḥ*, [and] *sthāt*;

[and in] *hi siñca*, *tu siñca*, *rajaḥsu sīdan*, *itaḥ siñcata*, *abhi sataḥ*, *kim u svit*, *sūrabhiḥ syāma*, *divit santu*, *ke stha*, *prati sphura*, *trī sadhasthā*, *kam u svit*,

nor in *vājī stutaḥ*, *vahanti sīm*, *patiḥ syām*, *ditsasi*, *stutaḥ*, *apo su myakṣa*, *śrudhi su*, *triḥ sma*, [and] *stuhi stuhi*.

Inside a word, [s] preceded by even-positioned consonants, semivowels, or alveolars occurring within the word undergoes retroflexion;

even when preceded by other sounds, if it shares in the word-beginning;

and [s] preceded by e, r, or *pṛtanā*;

when followed by r, ṛ, or ṝ, [it remains] unaltered by nature;

and [so too] in the syllables *sam*, *spṛk*, *svṛ*, *sar*, and *svar*;

and in its *sa*, if its penultimate has become retroflexed;

and also in a consonant cluster whose first element is nasal;

and no [retroflexion occurs in] *abhisatvā*, *rayisthānaḥ*, *yāsisīṣṭhāḥ*, *sisakṣi*, *tistire*, *tistirāṇā*, *sisice*, and *sisicuḥ*;

[but change occurs in] *goṣṭhāt iva*, *goṣatamāḥ*, *upaṣṭut*, *sapravādaḥ*, *nārṣadaḥ*, *paryaṣasvajat*, *svāduṣaṃsadaḥ*, the word *puruṣanti-*, *suṣaṃsadam*, *suṣamidhā*, [and] *anu seṣidhat*.

At a t, the final sound of a preceding word, preceded by an altering vowel, is modified when combining without r;

in the analysis (*vigraha*), however, before the two grave words *tvā* and *te*;

[in] *agniḥ*, at the beginning of a monosyllable;

*niḥ* before the following [words]: *tat*, *tatanyuḥ*, *tatakṣuḥ*, *tam*, and *taugryam*;

after *pāyubhiḥ*, *partṛbhiḥ*, *tribhiḥ*, *dadiḥ*, *veḥ*, *asmayuḥ*, and *śuciḥ*, before the following *tvam*;

[in] *īyuḥ te*, *vāvṛdhuḥ te*, *sadhiḥ tava*, *gobhiḥ tarema*, [and] *kratuḥ tam*;

not [in] *āhuḥ niṣṣidhvarīḥ*, *prabhoḥ*, *vandāruḥ*, *ṣaṣṭhiḥ*, *āviḥ*, *triḥ*, and *bāhvoḥ* before the two grave words.

The sounds ṛ, r, and ṣ retroflex an n in the same word or in a separable compound element (*avagṛhya*), even when situated within the word and preceded by non-k sounds, as well as in sandhi combinations;

a spirant formed by sandhi also [retroflexes an n] in an unanalyzable word (*aniṅgya*).

[They do] not [alter it when it is] separated by the intermediate classes of stops.

And in paripra-, ṛṣīndra-, and so forth, [not even when separated] by the last [consonant of a class].

Likewise [not when] separated by ś-sound or s-sound, in sarva- and so forth.

Except in expressions beginning with nābhinirṇik and the like;

[nor when it is] combined with a y-sound or a stop;

[nor] before niṣṭhā in karman, nor in dīrghanītha;

[nor] when followed by a short [vowel] in expressions beginning with tveṣapūrva;

and in triśubhra-, yuṣmad- and so forth, [nor] when followed by either [a short or long vowel];

[nor] in stems with an a-sound and in words of more than three syllables, and in words beginning with puraḥ-, punaḥ-, duḥ-, catur-, and jyotiḥ-.

[N-sound changes to ṇ-sound in] usrayāmṇe, anusrayāmṇe, suṣāmṇe, vṛṣamaṇyavaḥ, adhiṣavaṇyā, [and] praṇyaḥ;

in forms like dūḍhī, dūṇāśa, and dūlabha, the syllable duḥ which has become dū is the altering agent among them.

Unseparated in the resolution of the compound by obstructing [sounds], an r-sound and a spirant, occurring initially of all, [cause alteration] as stated;

[so also before] ānīt, nu tyam, nonuvuḥ, nonumaḥ, and verbs meaning 'to lead', when preceded by pra and pari;

before purupriyā, brahma suteṣu neṣi, [a form] ending in a protracted vowel with a ṣ-sound, indra, in nate, su sma in savaneṣu parṣi, [and with] svaḥ, aryamā, pra, uru, [and] pari, by these naḥ [is altered];

[It alters] in goroheṇa, nirgamāṇi, indra eṇā, indra eṇam, svarṇa, parāṇudasva, agne raveṇa, vārṇa, [and] śakra eṇam.

This is cerebralization: the transformation of dental into retroflex.

Preceded by a vowel or anusvāra, the beginning of a consonant cluster is pronounced twice; that is doubling when no krama [step-recitation occurs].

An aspirated stop, however, is pronounced once with its preceding [unaspirated stop] and once with its own [sound].

Even a ch-sound that does not stand at the beginning of a consonant cluster [is doubled].

or an ungeminated spirant in a cluster.

A spirant followed by a vowel or a spirant is not [doubled].

At the beginning of a word, preceded by saha, atihāya, pavamāna, yasya dve, and tane ca, the sound ch [is doubled];

and after a long [vowel], except for mā;

according to the Śākala [view], however, any consonant cluster [is doubled].

A final ṅ-sound and n-sound preceded by a short vowel are doubled when followed by a vowel.

Unless otherwise specified, in this chapter one should understand that the rule applies everywhere, even to secondary [derived] sounds.

The retention of stops and semivowels combined in sandhi—excluding the r-sound—and the checking of sound when stops follow, is checking (abhinidhāna);

Semivowels when followed by their own kinds, and also when nasalized [are checked].

According to the Śākala [view], an l-sound also before spirants [is checked];

and likewise a k-sound before a kh-sound derived from the root khyā;

Final stops followed by semivowels, r-sound, or spirants, and stops in word-initial position after an m-sound [are checked],

not, however, in a pada-end when su follows.

According to some, everywhere, or when there is a difference of organ and place of articulation, according to the Śākala [view].

Non-nasal stops [produce] transitional nasals of their own class before nasal stops.

A transitional nasal is naturally similar [to its original stop].

Or its sound with the transitional nasal is the primary one, having equal duration;

it is not different, however, from the original sound for the purpose of meaning.

A vowel glide does not destroy a consonant cluster.

According to Gārgya, a nasal vowel glide follows after a transitional nasal;

After checking (abhinidhāna), there is voice; it is fixed, occupying that same time and place of articulation;

[and possessing] the place of articulation of the nose if after a nasal.

The u-vowel of the word makṣu is lengthened everywhere, even when not at the end of a word.

In words other than sutā yāhi, [the vowel is lengthened] in the analysis [of] acha.

And the final ya of a following two-syllabled word having the acute (udātta) initially, not preceded by ā [is lengthened];

[and] that of na asya which is preceded by a consonant.

In niyūya, piṣṭatamayā, abhipadya, prāsya, saṅgatya, anudṛśya, abhivṛtya, ārabhya, sammīlya, makṣuṅgamābhiḥ, abhivlagya, yatra niṣadya, and vi;

[and in] nahi, jahi, abhi vīryeṇa, kṛdhi, and kṛṇutha—these before a monosyllabic word when subject to the kṣaipra glide;

hi before yukṣva, mandasva, [and] vidma;

su before juhota, yaja, dhāsatha, śiśīta, [and] bhara;

adha when su, nu, [or] hi follow;

vi before tṛmpa, ṛtena, muñcata, [and] adya;

the u-vowel forming the word su [or] nu when not followed by ari;

after those two, yoja and gha;

[and bhara] before mṛḷayadbhyām, vasuvittamam, yat, somam, jātavedasam, [and] bharata;

tena before sahasyena, suśravasam, pavasva, dve, [and] naḥ adhi;

dadhāta before devam, venam, [and] ketam;

śrudhi and vaṃsva before naḥ;

veda followed by viśvasya, bhṛmam, [and] ma.

7.41 And in the [hymn of] Śunaḥśepa, when followed by a y-sound, it is lengthened.

7.42 [The vowel is lengthened] in brahma before naḥ, and in the two instances of giraḥ kṛṇoti te, and when followed by kṛṇota and tūtot;

7.43 In abhi before naḥ, nu navante, sataḥ, naram, dvā, and sat su;

7.45 [Lengthening occurs before] cakruḥ, vadete, daśasyan, samudraḥ, rathena naḥ, sapta ṛṣīn, madanti, te vaḥ, bhayante, niyudbhiḥ, kṛpīṭam, rathasya, somasya, matī, raṇanti,

8.1 Inside a verse-quarter (pāda), when standing separate, the monoliteral element (apṛkta) u-vowel [is unlengthened in]: vrajasya, su, dhā, namobhiḥ, śucim, pavitram, but mahīḥ, nu, and not lengthened before sutasya;

