9.48 'sāhan', 'sāhāḥ', 'jarhṛṣanta', 'prasāham', 'naktoṣāsā', 'sūryam uṣāsam agnim', 'parirāpaḥ', 'sūnṛte jārayantī', 'śuśrūyātam', 'yūyuviḥ', and 'sādanā te';

9.49 'karan suṣāhā', 'ghṛtavānti', 'sāhvān', 'ṛjūya iva', 'sūyavasāt', 'vṛṣāya', 'uṣāsānaktā', 'pṛthujāghane ca', 'rāthyebhiḥ', 'rīriṣata', and 'glāpayanti';

9.50 'adhvānayat', 'rīriṣat', 'prāvaṇebhiḥ', 'rathīyanti iva', 'adamāyaḥ', 'sasāhe', 'sāsāha', 'yūyudhiḥ iva', 'aśrathāyaḥ', 'pūruṣaghnam', 'rīriṣaḥ', and 'pūruṣādaḥ';

11|49 or the reverse, according to those who hold otherwise.

11|50 Then, for both, when a nasalized [sound] follows;

11|51 and then when a nasalized [sound] is preceded by a retroflexed sound.

11|52 Likewise is the step-recitation (krama) of a syllable falling alone.

11|53 And here the preceding [word] would not obtain continuous text (saṃhitā) with a circumflex (svarita), / when that [following word] has a fixed vowel following.

11|54 And as the grave (anudātta) syllable comes under the influence / of the following word-initial, and thereby

11|55 when preceded by an acute (udātta) and followed by a fixed vowel, / the subsequent elision is indefinite when it is lower;

11|56 and when it depresses a subsequent part of the circumflex (svarita) vowel, / [being] a causeless syllable;

11|57 when even a conjunction of grave (anudātta) syllables preceded by an acute (udātta) / receives two or even many vowels.

11|59 If what is unseen in the Ṛṣi-text is seen in the step-recitation (krama), / others likewise call it an elision; / he, performing step-recitation (krama) without eliding the Ṛṣi-text, combining causes, even unites many.

preceding sas, ās, aus, and sampad-endings, likewise with a non-altering vowel as penultimate, if the sound y or the sound v follows before, not arising from euphonic combination;

also in words uttered like: jighāṃsan, pāṃsure, māṃsam, pumāṃsam, pauṃsyam;

nor after praśliṣṭa or abhinihita [sandhi];

nor in māṃcatve, ayāṃsam.

This much is the anusvāra following a long vowel in the verses (ṛcs).

The six modifications following the letter p—rādhaḥ, rathaḥ, gnāḥ, divaḥ, jā, and ṛtaḥ—and the two initial words añjaḥpā and duḥpra: one should make a disjunction (vikrama) in these without separating [the components].

The teachers Vyāḍi, Śākalya, and Gārgya call that which is to be brought about: the change to ṣ, likewise the change to ṇ, euphonic combinations conditioned by euphony, and the substitution of visarga established by rule.

Some say the penultimate short vowel before an anusvāra is incomplete by half a vowel glide; anusvāra preceded by a short vowel is augmented by just that much;

They state that there is a distinction of function in different tempos;

a distinction of mora occurs with each tempo.

One should employ the fast tempo for study, the moderate for recitation, and the slow tempo for instructing pupils.

The blue jay utters one mora; the crow has spoken two morae; the peacock is known to have three morae: this is the comprehension of a mora.

The qualities of speech-sounds have been declared before, and what belongs to the continuous text (saṃhitā); their addition, omission, and corruption are faults: we shall explain them here by way of demonstration.

The thrown-out (nirasta) [fault occurs] when the place of articulation and active articulator are retracted;

over-expansion (vyāsa) and over-compression (pīḍana) [occur] in excessive opening and closing [of the mouth];

bound up (naddha) and defective (duṣṭa) are uttered as mumbled (ambūkṛta) by the lips;

swollen (śūna) [is uttered] with a hollow mouth;

clenched (sandaṣṭa), however, is uttered by pressing the jaws together;

they call it torn apart (vikliṣṭa) when drawn out excessively;

swallowed (grasta) is this [fault resulting] from constriction of the root of the tongue;

nasalized [in excess], however, [arises] from attachment to the nostrils;

pronunciation of vowels not according to their proper mora [is also a fault].
